# Evaluation-harness configuration. Copy to `.env` (gitignored) and tune.
# Only `make eval` (the live run) reads these; `make eval-selftest` is cost-free.
# See docs/evaluation/evaluation-harness/evaluation-harness.md for the contract.
#
# THIS FILE BELONGS TO THE MAIN CHECKOUT. A repository has exactly one `.env`
# and it lives there (ADR 0014, docs/decisions/0014-one-env-per-repository.md).
# From a linked git worktree every reader resolves, first hit wins:
#   1. HARNESS_ENV_FILE (the harness and the bench), LANGFUSE_ENV_FILE (the
#      Langfuse scripts) or HELDOUT_ENV_FILE (the held-out benchmark) — an
#      explicit path, which need not exist;
#   2. this checkout's own `.env`;
#   3. the MAIN checkout's `.env`, found as the parent of git's common dir,
#      READ IN PLACE and named once on stderr. It is never copied.
# So a worktree never holds a secret: `make set-key` run from one writes the
# main checkout's file and says so. An exported variable still beats the file
# (`HARNESS_PROVIDER=local make eval` wins), and every selftest runs with both
# overrides pointed at /dev/null, so `make check` never reads this file.

# --- Provider ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Which backend the flow's `claude -p` calls hit:
#   anthropic  (default) — your Claude Code login, or ANTHROPIC_API_KEY below.
#   openrouter — routes through OpenRouter's Anthropic-compatible endpoint so you
#     can run FREE / open models. Set OPENROUTER_API_KEY (write it safely with
#     `make set-key PROVIDER=openrouter`), then put OpenRouter model ids in
#     HARNESS_MODELS. Free tier is ~20 req/min — keep HARNESS_CONCURRENCY low.
#   local — routes to a model server on YOUR machine (Ollama is the documented
#     reference; anything serving an Anthropic-shaped /v1/messages works by changing
#     one URL). No credential, $0 per token, no 429/529 to survive. See the
#     `provider=local` block below and
#     docs/evaluation/local-model-baseline/local-model-baseline.md.
HARNESS_PROVIDER="anthropic"
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY="sk-ant-api-..."     # provider=anthropic: a DEDICATED org key
# OPENROUTER_API_KEY="sk-or-v1-..."      # provider=openrouter: your OpenRouter key
# HARNESS_OPENROUTER_BASE_URL="https://openrouter.ai/api"   # override only if needed

# --- provider=local (a model server on this machine) -------------------------
# There is no secret here, so `make set-key` deliberately has no `local` provider.
# Instead of a credential gate, `make eval` runs a PREFLIGHT that refuses to start
# on: an unreachable endpoint, a model that was never pulled, or a context window
# below the floor — each with a fix-it message, and each skipped under a dry run so
# `make check` never touches localhost.
# HARNESS_LOCAL_BASE_URL="http://localhost:11434"   # the model server
# HARNESS_LOCAL_API_KEY="local"     # DUMMY — the CLI needs a non-empty token; local
#                                   # servers ignore its value. Not a secret.
# HARNESS_LOCAL_MIN_CONTEXT="32768" # hard floor, asserted by the preflight
# HARNESS_LOCAL_TIMEOUT="10"        # seconds per preflight probe
# HARNESS_LOCAL_CLAUDE_CONTEXT=""   # item 25.1: the window Claude Code is told for
#                                   # a local run (CLAUDE_CODE_MAX_CONTEXT_TOKENS,
#                                   # exported for provider=local only). Unset = not
#                                   # exported (Claude Code assumes 200,000). The
#                                   # value is a threshold choice; item 25.2 picks it.
# HARNESS_COST_PER_RUN_LOCAL="0.00" # $0 per token — see the honesty note below
#
# CONTEXT WINDOW IS THE #1 REPRODUCIBILITY TRAP. A runtime commonly sizes the
# served window from free VRAM and then TRUNCATES SILENTLY: on a 16GB M1, a model
# advertising a 262144-token maximum was observed being SERVED at 4096. Every agent
# run would still produce plausible-looking rows. The preflight therefore checks the
# model's advertised ceiling AND — once the model is loaded — the window the server
# is ACTUALLY serving (`/api/ps`). Set it explicitly before a run:
#   OLLAMA_CONTEXT_LENGTH=32768 ollama serve      # or bake `num_ctx` into a Modelfile
#
# SERIAL IS REQUIRED, not advisory: a single local GPU/CPU serialises anyway, and
# HARNESS_CONCURRENCY>1 causes swap/VRAM thrash that corrupts the latency numbers
# which are the whole point of measuring a $0 model. Keep HARNESS_CONCURRENCY="1".
#
# Recipe — SMOKE (one easy fixture, one flow, N=3; the escalation gate):
#   HARNESS_PROVIDER=local HARNESS_FLOWS=role-specific-chain \
#   HARNESS_TASKS=feature-slugify HARNESS_MODELS=<your-runtime-tag> \
#   HARNESS_CONCURRENCY=1 HARNESS_RUNS_PER_TASK=3 HARNESS_CONFIRM=1 make eval
#
# Recipe — FULL SUITE (12 tasks, one flow, N=3 — only after the smoke passes;
# budget hours of local compute, and note the expected wall-clock before starting):
#   HARNESS_PROVIDER=local HARNESS_FLOWS=role-specific-chain \
#   HARNESS_MODELS=<your-runtime-tag> HARNESS_CONCURRENCY=1 \
#   HARNESS_RUNS_PER_TASK=3 HARNESS_CONFIRM=1 make eval && make eval-report

# --- Model axis (second experimental axis) ----------------------------------
# For HARNESS_PROVIDER=openrouter, set these to OpenRouter model ids instead of the
# Anthropic classes below. For HARNESS_PROVIDER=local, set them to your runtime's
# TAGS (e.g. `qwen3.8`, `qwen3.8:27b-q4_K_M`); leaving HARNESS_MODELS unset under
# local runs the pinned `qwen3.8`. Pin the exact variant + quantisation you pulled —
# `qwen3.8` is a VERSION, not a parameter count, and the tag is what makes a
# scorecard reproducible. Leaving HARNESS_MODELS UNSET under openrouter runs the
# pinned free eval set (nvidia/nemotron-3-ultra-550b-a55b:free +
# nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b:free). To pin them explicitly:
#   HARNESS_MODELS="nvidia/nemotron-3-ultra-550b-a55b:free nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b:free"
# HARNESS_MODELS is the sweep list and WINS when set. It defaults to all three
# pinned classes, so a live `make eval` sweeps the full model matrix — roughly
# 3x the cost of a single-model run (owned by the printed estimate + confirm gate).
# Precedence: HARNESS_MODELS (if set) > HARNESS_MODEL > built-in three-class default.
HARNESS_MODELS="claude-haiku-4-5-20251001 claude-sonnet-5 claude-opus-4-8"

# Single-model fallback — used only when HARNESS_MODELS is unset. The simple,
# back-compatible path (score one model, no sweep).
HARNESS_MODEL="claude-sonnet-5"

# --- Flow axis --------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional flow filter: a space/comma list of flow-slugs from scripts/harness/flows.tsv
# to scope a paid run to a subset. Empty/unset = all runnable flows (full sweep).
# The worked example scopes to the single chain, e.g.:
#   HARNESS_FLOWS="role-specific-chain"   -> 1 flow x 3 tasks x 3 models x N = 27 runs
HARNESS_FLOWS=""

# --- Task axis --------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional task filter (mirrors HARNESS_FLOWS): a space/comma list of task SLUGS
# and/or TIER names (easy | medium | hard | expert). A tier name expands to every
# task at that COMPUTED complexity tier (see the task-complexity-rubric doc), so a
# paid sweep can be scoped to just the discriminating tasks. Empty/unset = all.
#   HARNESS_TASKS="hard"                          -> only the hard-tier fixtures
#   HARNESS_TASKS="hard expert"                   -> the two top tiers
#   HARNESS_TASKS="complexity-rpn-eval"           -> a single named fixture
# An unknown slug-or-tier is a wiring error caught by `make eval-selftest`.
HARNESS_TASKS=""

# --- Reproducibility --------------------------------------------------------
# N runs per (flow x task x model); results are reported as mean +/- range since
# agent runs are non-deterministic (Claude Code exposes no seed/temperature).
HARNESS_RUNS_PER_TASK="3"

# Concurrency — how many matrix cells run at once. Cells are independent (each in
# its own throwaway git worktree with independent grading), so >1 cuts wall-clock;
# 1 (default) is strictly serial. Rows are written per-cell and concatenated in
# matrix order, so results.jsonl is identical regardless of this value. Higher
# values contend for the same Claude rate limit (the durable per-cell pause/retry
# still applies), so raise it gradually. e.g. HARNESS_CONCURRENCY="4".
# Under HARNESS_PROVIDER=local this must stay "1" — see the provider=local block.
HARNESS_CONCURRENCY="1"

# Run only a fixed PERCENTAGE of the (already HARNESS_TASKS-narrowed) catalog — a
# cheap smoke sample to check the harness end to end. 1..100 (default 100 = all).
# Selection is deterministic (the first ceil(total * pct / 100) tasks by name),
# always at least one task. Stacks with HARNESS_TASKS.
HARNESS_TASK_PERCENT="100"

# --- Output format ----------------------------------------------------------
# `stream-json` (default) captures the full JSONL trajectory so metrics.sh counts
# REAL tool-use events and derives the self-correction proxy; cost/tokens still
# come from the final result event. Set to `json` to fall back to the summary
# envelope (tool_calls/self_corrections then degrade to null, never a false 0).
# HARNESS_OUTPUT_FORMAT="stream-json"

# --- Cost gate --------------------------------------------------------------
# `make eval` prints an estimated cost of the WHOLE matrix and refuses to spend
# unless this is exactly "1". Leave unset/empty to preview the estimate only.
HARNESS_CONFIRM=""

# Coarse per-class rough $/run fallbacks for the pre-run estimate. They are ONLY
# consulted when no results/ snapshot exists to estimate from (once you have run
# once, the estimate uses the mean observed cost per model instead). Rough, not
# a promise — the real total_cost_usd is captured per run from the JSON envelope.
HARNESS_COST_PER_RUN_HAIKU="0.05"
HARNESS_COST_PER_RUN_SONNET="0.30"
HARNESS_COST_PER_RUN_OPUS="1.00"
# provider=local: $0 per token by construction, so the estimate prints ~$0.00. Read
# that as "$0 API", NOT as "free" — the real price is wall-clock and hardware, and
# cost-per-solve degenerates at $0 (latency and throughput are the economic axis).
HARNESS_COST_PER_RUN_LOCAL="0.00"

# --- Durable rate-limit handling --------------------------------------------
# A live sweep can be long and unattended, so a cell that hits a Claude rate-limit
# or overload PAUSES and retries instead of failing. Every pause/resume is logged
# (timestamped) to results/<ts>/rate-limit.log AND stderr, so a genuine throttle
# pause is distinguishable from a hang. Each row also carries a `rate_limit_pauses`
# count. Genuine (non-throttle) errors are NOT retried — the cell is recorded as a
# failed run rather than spinning.
#
# Halt deadline: a cell retries through throttling until it clears OR it has been
# rate-limited CONTINUOUSLY for this many hours — then the run halts (exit 75,
# EX_TEMPFAIL) so an automation wrapper can retry the whole run later rather than
# hang indefinitely. 0 = never halt (retry forever). Default 8h.
HARNESS_RATELIMIT_DEADLINE_HOURS="8"
# Optional attempt-COUNT cap per cell (0 = no count cap; the time deadline is the
# real bound). Set a finite value to also bound the number of retries.
HARNESS_RATELIMIT_MAX_RETRIES="0"
# Retry cadence is split by throttle KIND:
#   * 429 (your account rate limit) / generic — a FIXED interval, since your
#     per-minute quota refills steadily; a constant cadence resumes as soon as it
#     clears (lower HARNESS_CONCURRENCY also helps 429s). Legacy name
#     HARNESS_RATELIMIT_BASE_WAIT still accepted.
HARNESS_RATELIMIT_WAIT="60"
#   * 529 (Anthropic-side overload) — EXPONENTIAL backoff (base doubling, capped),
#     the documented handling for server overload; concurrency does NOT affect 529.
HARNESS_RATELIMIT_529_BASE_WAIT="30"
HARNESS_RATELIMIT_529_MAX_WAIT="600"

# --- OpenRouter free-tier baseline recipes (item 8) -------------------------
# The survey/smoke/baseline steps of docs/evaluation/openrouter-free-tier-baseline/
# reuse the knobs above — no new required variable. `survey_models.sh` (behind
# `make eval-models`) also reads OPENROUTER_API_KEY (set it with
# `make set-key PROVIDER=openrouter`). The three steps, as inline-scoped runs:
#
#   # 1. Survey the live free-tier field (cost-free; renders the dated table):
#   make eval-models
#
#   # 2. Smoke-filter the :free candidates (one easy fixture, serial, N=3):
#   HARNESS_PROVIDER=openrouter HARNESS_FLOWS=role-specific-chain \
#   HARNESS_TASKS=feature-slugify \
#   HARNESS_MODELS="<:free candidate ids from the survey>" \
#   HARNESS_CONCURRENCY=1 HARNESS_RUNS_PER_TASK=3 HARNESS_CONFIRM=1 make eval
#
#   # 3. Baseline the top-1 survivor over the full 12-task suite (N=3):
#   HARNESS_PROVIDER=openrouter HARNESS_FLOWS=role-specific-chain \
#   HARNESS_MODELS="<survivor id>" \
#   HARNESS_RUNS_PER_TASK=3 HARNESS_CONFIRM=1 make eval && make eval-report

# --- Advanced ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Extra flags passed to each `claude -p` invocation (headless file edits need a
# non-interactive permission mode). Override only if you know why.
# HARNESS_CLAUDE_ARGS="--permission-mode acceptEdits"

# The results root (item 31) for make eval, make bench and make coding-bench,
# and for the reports that read the latest snapshot: results/<ts>/,
# results/bench/<ts>/ and the survey dirs all live under it. Unset, it is the
# checkout's results/ (gitignored). The selftests point it at their own scratch
# dir, so concurrent runs never delete or read each other's dry-run output.
# AISDLC_RESULTS_DIR="/path/to/results"

# ============================================================================
# Local inference benchmark (make bench*) — item 10.
# Only `make bench` / `make bench-report` / `make bench-models` read these;
# `make bench-selftest` (in `make check`) passes its own knobs inline. Defaults
# below are the ones bench_defaults in scripts/bench/bench_lib.sh applies; the
# inline environment wins over this file. See
# docs/evaluation/inference-benchmark/inference-benchmark.md for the contract.
# ============================================================================

# --- Gate -------------------------------------------------------------------
# `make bench` prints the plan (host class, engines detected/skipped, stage-1
# config count, estimated minutes, GB to download) and refuses to start unless
# this is exactly "1". Leave unset/empty to preview the plan only.
BENCH_CONFIRM=""

# Whole-run wall-clock budget in minutes. Checked before every config; on expiry
# the in-flight config is finished, its server stopped, never-timed configs get a
# run=0 refused row and the recommendation is marked partial: true.
BENCH_BUDGET_MIN="180"

# --- Artifacts --------------------------------------------------------------
# Where model files live — OUTSIDE the repo (no .gitignore change needed). Left
# commented because harness_load_env does not expand $HOME; bench_lib expands a
# leading `~/` itself and defaults to this value in code.
# BENCH_MODEL_CACHE="~/.cache/ai-sdlc-bench"

# Cap on the cache size in GB. pull.sh refuses a download (status=refused,
# note=disk-budget) rather than exceed it, and refuses (disk-free) when the
# filesystem would drop under 2 GB free. Nothing is ever evicted.
BENCH_DISK_BUDGET_GB="20"

# --- Host headroom ----------------------------------------------------------
# RAM (GB) kept back from the band rule at plan time and from the memory-headroom
# precondition (peak_mem_gb <= ram_gb - reserve, or <= vram_gb on a discrete GPU).
BENCH_RAM_RESERVE_GB="4"

# --- Recommender preconditions ----------------------------------------------
# context_served floor — the same >=32k floor as item 9's HARNESS_LOCAL_MIN_CONTEXT
# and item 12; recommendation.env never renders below 32768 even if lowered here.
BENCH_MIN_CONTEXT="32768"
# 1 = a config whose tool-call probe fails is excluded (reason `tools`).
BENCH_REQUIRE_TOOLS="1"
# Max swap growth (MB) during a run; 0 = "fits without swap" (reason `swap`).
BENCH_MAX_SWAP_MB="0"
# The pick's decode floor (tok/s) — an agentic-usability floor; frontier points
# below it are kept and flagged meets_min_decode:false, never picked.
BENCH_MIN_DECODE_TPS="15"

# --- Scope ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional engine filter (space/comma list of llamacpp|ollama|vllm|mlx); empty =
# every engine detected. An unknown token exits 3.
BENCH_ENGINES=""
# Optional model filter: models.tsv `id` and/or `family` tokens (a family token
# such as "qwen3.5" selects every artifact of that family across engines).
# Empty = every row that fits the band rule. An unknown token exits 3.
BENCH_MODELS=""

# --- Runner -----------------------------------------------------------------
# The one port every adapter serves on (127.0.0.1 only); asserted free before
# every serve and after every stop.
BENCH_PORT="18080"
# N timed runs per config (mean and min-max range reported; >= 1).
BENCH_RUNS="3"
# Stage-1 configs per engine refined in stage 2, one knob at a time (0 = stage 1 only).
BENCH_TOPK="3"
# Seconds to wait for GET /v1/models to answer 200 after serve.sh starts a server.
BENCH_SERVE_TIMEOUT_S="300"
# curl -m for every measured request and the tool probe (a 65k-context prefill
# on CPU can be slow).
BENCH_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_S="600"

# --- Dry run ----------------------------------------------------------------
# 1 = the mock path (fixtures, synthetic rows; bench_curl / bench_spawn refuse and
# count). This is what `make bench-selftest` sets; leave empty for a real scan.
BENCH_DRY_RUN=""

# --- Unified-memory hosts, hybrid and speculative models (ADR 0006, item 13) -
# Every knob below is empty by default, and empty renders exactly the item-10
# plan, serve command, config_id and recommendation. See
# docs/decisions/0006-bench-first-class-apu-serving.md.
#
# Host memory overrides. detect_host.sh writes memory_topology (unified for Apple
# Silicon and AMD/Intel iGPUs, from sysfs) and gtt_gb into host.json; on a unified
# host the band rule budgets ram_gb + vram_gb − BENCH_RAM_RESERVE_GB AND
# vram_gb + gtt_gb (minus the model's host-resident bytes), not the BIOS carve.
# 1 = force unified, 0 = force discrete, empty = host.json.
BENCH_UNIFIED_MEMORY=""
# Replaces host.json vram_gb (decimal GB), e.g. to plan for another BIOS carve.
BENCH_VRAM_GB=""
# Architecture table for the arch-aware KV estimate (default scripts/bench/arch.tsv).
# BENCH_ARCH_TSV="scripts/bench/arch.tsv"
#
# Stage-1 context axis for every engine (space/comma list), replacing each
# profile.tsv `context` row, e.g. "8192 32768 131072 262144". Empty = profiles.
BENCH_CONTEXTS=""
# Stage-1 KV cache types (item 23.1; space/comma list), replacing the stage-1
# kv value (f16) of every engine, e.g. "q8_0" or "f16 q8_0" (the grid doubles).
# Kept: the -ctk/-ctv types llama.cpp accepts (f32 f16 bf16 q8_0 q4_0 q4_1
# q5_0 q5_1 iq4_nl); anything else is dropped. config_id reads -kv<type>; a
# quantised V cache gets -fa on from serve.sh, so the id has no -fa suffix.
# Empty = f16, the profiles' stage-1 value.
BENCH_KVS=""
# Stage-1 offload axis (item 32.1; space/comma list of all | auto | cpu),
# replacing every profile's `offload` row the way BENCH_CONTEXTS replaces the
# context axis, e.g. "auto" halves a scan when only the pinned offload matters.
# Anything else is dropped. Empty = the profiles' offload rows.
BENCH_OFFLOADS=""
# Measure prefill, decode and the multi-turn replay AT a prompt depth: a token
# count, or `ctx` (the config's context − max(1536, context × 3/64)). The
# prompt is filled from scripts/bench/prompts/depth-corpus.txt; rows record
# prompt_depth. The replay's turns then sit on the same prefix, so turn 1 pays
# the cold prefill and turn N measures reuse at depth. Empty = the item-10
# long-8k / generate-256 prompts and the ~1.2k-token replay.
BENCH_PROMPT_DEPTH=""
#
# Serving knobs (llama.cpp; profile.tsv `meta knobs`). Each departs from the
# default only when set, and the non-default ones are appended to config_id as
# -ub<n>, -mtp<n>, -rb<n>, -cr<n> in that order, then item 32.1's -mtpa<n>,
# one of -ngm/-ngs/-ngk with the effective sizes, and -pm<hundredths>.
# -ub <n>; lifts serve.sh's 512 micro-batch cap only when set.
BENCH_UBATCH=""
# none | mtp — speculative decoding with the model's MTP head (--spec-type draft-mtp)
# | mtp-adaptive (item 32.1: --spec-type draft-mtp-adaptive, the fork's adaptive
# depth; BENCH_DRAFT_N is then its ceiling, and config_id reads -mtpa<n>).
BENCH_DRAFT=""
# The draft model for BENCH_DRAFT=mtp: <org>/<repo>/<file.gguf> (pulled by
# engines/llamacpp/pull.sh into the cache) or an absolute path; empty = the
# target GGUF's own MTP head. E.g.
# agentionai/Qwen3.8-Flash-Next-MTP-Q8_0-GGUF/Qwen3.8-Flash-Next-MTP-Q8_0.gguf (rev f843615d).
BENCH_DRAFT_ARTIFACT=""
# --spec-draft-n-max <n> (4 when BENCH_DRAFT=mtp or mtp-adaptive and this is empty).
BENCH_DRAFT_N=""
# One draftless speculator joined into the same --spec-type list (item 32.1):
# ngram-mod | ngram-simple | ngram-map-k4v. Allowed with BENCH_DRAFT=none (then
# no draft model is loaded). Anything else is refused by name, never dropped.
BENCH_SPEC_EXTRA=""
# Its sizes, each rendered only when set and refused unless BENCH_SPEC_EXTRA is
# that type; config_id carries the EFFECTIVE sizes (the fork's defaults filled
# in): -ngm<match>.<min>.<max> (24/48/64), -ngs<n>.<m> (12/48), -ngk<n>.<m>.<hits>
# (12/48/1). 1..1024 (n-min 0..1024); anything else means empty.
BENCH_NGRAM_MOD_N_MATCH=""
BENCH_NGRAM_MOD_N_MIN=""
BENCH_NGRAM_MOD_N_MAX=""
BENCH_NGRAM_SIMPLE_SIZE_N=""
BENCH_NGRAM_SIMPLE_SIZE_M=""
BENCH_NGRAM_K4V_SIZE_N=""
BENCH_NGRAM_K4V_SIZE_M=""
BENCH_NGRAM_K4V_MIN_HITS=""
# --spec-draft-p-min <p>: the MTP draft stops below this top-1 probability
# (item 32.1). 0..1, normalised to two decimals (0.5 -> 0.50); -pm<hundredths>
# in config_id. Refused without an MTP draft.
BENCH_DRAFT_P_MIN=""
# --reasoning-effort low|medium|high (recorded in the row, not in config_id).
BENCH_REASONING_EFFORT=""
# --reasoning-budget <tokens> (0 = no thinking); appended to config_id as -rb<n>.
BENCH_REASONING_BUDGET=""
# --ctx-checkpoints <n>: recurrent-state checkpoints for multi-turn prefix reuse
# on hybrid models (recorded in serve metadata, not in config_id).
BENCH_CTX_CHECKPOINTS=""
# --cache-ram <MiB>: llama-server's host-RAM prompt cache (item 23.1). Empty =
# no flag, i.e. llama.cpp's own default of 8192 MiB. 0 = no host prompt cache,
# and 0 is kept (unlike BENCH_UBATCH); -1, a leading zero or anything else
# non-numeric means empty. Set means non-default: it is appended to config_id
# as -cr<n> after -rb, so an explicit 8192 is a distinct id from empty.
BENCH_CACHE_RAM=""
# --lazy-buffer-size <n>M (item 34.1): read qwen4exp's PLE n-gram table from
# disk on demand through an n MiB row cache instead of loading it. A positive
# MiB count; empty or 0 = the engine default (the table stays resident).
BENCH_LAZY_BUF=""
# Build identity of this scan, recorded as engine_build in scan.env, every row
# and recommendation.json: <engine>@<fork>/<commit>+<backend>, e.g.
# llamacpp@Nathanw1014/b02cb35f2+vulkan. One scan = one build (BENCH_LLAMACPP_BIN).
BENCH_ENGINE_BUILD=""
#
# Agentic metrics that cost extra requests (empty = not measured; the row key is
# then absent, never 0). The code prompt, the multi-turn replay, greedy_output_sha
# and, for any speculative config, the coherence gate (a draft-off restart) always run.
# tool_call_ok_rate over this many tool-call probes.
BENCH_TOOL_CALLS_N=""
# sustained_decode_tps over this many seconds of back-to-back decode.
BENCH_SUSTAINED_S=""
# 1 = the edit-replay prompt (item 32.1): a ~1.7k-token file returned by a
# read_file tool call, re-emitted with one small edit, thinking off. Adds
# decode_tps_edit per run and greedy_output_sha_gen / _edit per config; the edit
# sha joins the coherence gate, which then runs for ANY speculative config.
BENCH_EDIT_REPLAY=""

# --- Optional ---------------------------------------------------------------
# HF_TOKEN=""                    # Hugging Face token: higher rate limit for
#                                # `make bench-models` and pull.sh size checks
# BENCH_IGNORE_RUNNING="1"       # proceed although a foreign `ollama serve` is running
# BENCH_LLAMACPP_BIN="/path/to/llama-server"   # explicit binaries for detect.sh
# BENCH_OLLAMA_BIN="/path/to/ollama"           # (also BENCH_MLX_BIN, BENCH_VLLM_BIN)
# BENCH_SURVEY_SEED_QUANTS="unsloth/Qwen3.8-Flash-Next-GGUF=UD-IQ4_XS,UD-Q4_K_XL"   # make bench-models:
#                                # per-seed quant list (<repo>=<label>[,<label>...])
# The gufo engine (item 45, scripts/bench/engines/gufo/): run from its container
# image under Docker, PINNED BY DIGEST (a tag is refused), never pulled by the
# bench. The default is the reviewed pin in engines/gufo/gufo_lib.sh.
# BENCH_GUFO_IMAGE="ghcr.io/gufo-org/toolboxes/gufo-runtime@sha256:<64 hex>"
# BENCH_GUFO_PORT="18090"        # the candidate's port (ADR 0019); never 18080. A
#                                # gufo scan serves on BENCH_PORT, so set that too.

# --- Consuming the recommendation -------------------------------------------
# After a scan (or `make bench-report`), append the pick to this file and start
# the recommended server with the printed command:
#   make bench-report
#   cat results/bench/<ts>/recommendation.env >> .env   # HARNESS_PROVIDER=local,
#       # HARNESS_LOCAL_BASE_URL, HARNESS_LOCAL_API_KEY, HARNESS_LOCAL_MIN_CONTEXT,
#       # HARNESS_MODELS, HARNESS_CONCURRENCY=1, BENCH_ENGINE, BENCH_CONFIG_ID,
#       # BENCH_SERVE_CMD
#   . .env && eval "$BENCH_SERVE_CMD" &
#   # then run item 9's local smoke (HARNESS_PROVIDER=local); the manual equivalent is
#   # `curl http://127.0.0.1:$BENCH_PORT/v1/models` (reachable, model present).

# --- LAN model host (item 11) ------------------------------------------------
# The model server need not be on this machine. Item 9's local provider takes any
# URL, so a LAN-served engine (bind/firewall/discovery recipes and the agent-harness
# configs in docs/evaluation/agent-harness/) runs the evaluation harness UNCHANGED:
#   HARNESS_LOCAL_BASE_URL="http://HOST:11434"   # HOST = the serving machine's LAN
#       # hostname or reserved IP; the preflight's reachability probe covers the LAN
# Recipes for driving an AGENT harness (OpenCode, mini-SWE-agent, ...) against the
# same server live in scripts/harness/agent-configs/<harness>/<engine>.env.

# =============================================================================
# Coding benchmark — harness x engine x model (item 12)   HARNESS_BENCH_*
# =============================================================================
# `make coding-bench` scores the full TRIPLE — agent harness + inference engine +
# model configuration — against established, execution-graded coding suites run
# LOCALLY on this machine. Nothing is imported from a leaderboard.
#
# Not to be confused with the BENCH_* block above (item 10), which scans
# inference ENGINES for throughput. Different script, different knobs, different
# results directory (results/bench/<ts>/ here, written by scripts/harness/).
#
# The resource this benchmark spends is WALL-CLOCK, not dollars. `make coding-bench`
# prints a projected wall-clock per triple and refuses to start without
# HARNESS_CONFIRM=1.

# --- Ladder rung ------------------------------------------------------------
# smoke    a handful of instances per suite — proves the wiring end to end and is
#          the ESCALATION GATE: only configurations whose smoke produced at least
#          one `ran` cell are worth a standard run.
# standard the COMMITTED scorecard (SWE-bench Lite · HumanEval+ · MBPP+ ·
#          LiveCodeBench's latest release window · BigCodeBench-Hard).
# full     SWE-bench Verified and the complete sets — documented, not required.
# Default is `smoke`, deliberately: `standard` is many hours.
# Named lists (item 20.5), SWE-bench Lite only, so set
# HARNESS_BENCH_SUITES=swe-bench-lite with them: item20-dev (15),
# item20-heldout (38, fixed order).
HARNESS_BENCH_PROFILE="smoke"

# --- Scope ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional suite filter (space/comma list). Empty = the DEFAULT sweep, the v1
# five (ADR 0023). Unknown -> exit 3. Any manifest suite may be named:
#   swe-bench-lite humanevalplus mbppplus livecodebench bigcodebench-hard
#   swe-bench-pro   (item 37 — manifest-only: runs ONLY when named here)
HARNESS_BENCH_SUITES=""
# Optional configuration filter: ids from scripts/harness/configs/*.toml
# (<harness>__<engine>__<model-tag>). Empty = the DEFAULT sweep: every
# committed profile except a `sweep = "named"` one, which runs ONLY when named
# here (item 12's gufo profile). Unknown -> exit 3. See
# scripts/harness/configs/README.md.
HARNESS_BENCH_CONFIGS=""

# --- Budget -----------------------------------------------------------------
# Advisory only: when the projected wall-clock exceeds this, the plan prints a
# warning. Unset = no warning. It never stops a confirmed run — the operator owns
# the decision, having been shown the number.
HARNESS_BENCH_BUDGET_HOURS=""
# The tok/s the pre-run projection assumes BEFORE any smoke has been measured.
# Once a results/bench/ snapshot exists the MEASURED mean replaces this.
HARNESS_BENCH_REFERENCE_TOK_S="8.5"
# Optional: renders a $-equivalent per solve (wall-clock x rate / solves) in the
# scorecard's cost table. Unset by default, and the column then reads
# `n/a (set rate)` rather than implying the run was free.
# HARNESS_BENCH_HW_RATE_USD_PER_HOUR="0.50"

# --- Engine mode (item 20.3, ADR 0015) --------------------------------------
# own      (default) each profile's engine is started and stopped by the run.
# managed  drive the MANAGED, PINNED server (scripts/serve/, item 22) as-is:
#          nothing is started, stopped or reconfigured. Preflight is
#          `serve.sh check` (pin, binary AND a live process all green), the
#          served config_hash goes on every row, and a hash that changes during
#          or between cells stops the run (exit 5; that cell's rows go to
#          quarantine.jsonl). Use it with a profile that describes the pin, e.g.
#          pi__llamacpp__qwen3.8-flash-next-125b-a6b-udiq4xs.
# HARNESS_BENCH_ENGINE_MODE="own"

# --- Pi arms (item 20.3) ----------------------------------------------------
# The pi-variants bundle one run uses: scripts/harness/bench/pi-variants/<name>/.
# Default `baseline` (empty by contract). An unknown name fails preflight
# rather than silently running the baseline. The name is written onto every
# trace as `variant`, and AISDLC_EXPERIMENT_ID (optional) as `experiment_id`,
# so signals.py can split and filter the arms.
# HARNESS_BENCH_PI_VARIANT="baseline"
# Per-instance wall-clock cap for one Pi run, in seconds (SIGINT, then SIGKILL
# 30 s later). usage.json records `timed_out`.
# BENCH_PI_TIMEOUT_S="1200"
# Extra ABSOLUTE directories the item 16 sandbox hides behind an empty tmpfs,
# colon-separated (item 38.2). The held-out runner sets it per instance to hide
# the store and the local transcript directories; unset, nothing changes.
# BENCH_SANDBOX_HIDE=""
#
# A traced run: AISDLC_TRACE_BENCH=1 keeps the LANGFUSE_* keys the bench
# otherwise unsets, and rows then carry traced:true. LANGFUSE_HOST is not in
# .env; `eval "$(scripts/langfuse/langfuse.sh env)"` exports all three.

# --- Rules that are NOT knobs ------------------------------------------------
# HARNESS_CONCURRENCY=1 is REQUIRED. A second in-flight cell shares the engine
# process, so tok/s, TTFT and peak memory stop meaning anything. run_bench.sh
# exits 2 on anything higher — this is item 9's local-model rule, not a default.
#
# The 32768-token context floor is asserted twice: against the profile's declared
# [engine] context_length, and against the context the engine actually SERVES.

# --- Recipes ----------------------------------------------------------------
# Smoke every committed triple (the escalation gate):
#   HARNESS_BENCH_PROFILE=smoke HARNESS_CONFIRM=1 make coding-bench
#
# Smoke one triple, one suite (fastest useful loop):
#   HARNESS_BENCH_PROFILE=smoke \
#   HARNESS_BENCH_SUITES=humanevalplus \
#   HARNESS_BENCH_CONFIGS=opencode__ollama__qwen3.5-9b-q4km \
#   HARNESS_CONFIRM=1 make coding-bench
#
# The committed standard scorecard (hours — read the projection first):
#   HARNESS_BENCH_PROFILE=standard HARNESS_BENCH_BUDGET_HOURS=24 \
#   HARNESS_CONFIRM=1 make coding-bench
#
# Re-render a scorecard without re-running anything:
#   make coding-bench-report
#
# One Pi arm against the managed server, traced (item 20.3; repeat per arm):
#   eval "$(scripts/langfuse/langfuse.sh env)"
#   AISDLC_TRACE_BENCH=1 AISDLC_EXPERIMENT_ID=<id> \
#   HARNESS_BENCH_ENGINE_MODE=managed HARNESS_BENCH_PI_VARIANT=baseline \
#   HARNESS_BENCH_SUITES=swe-bench-lite \
#   HARNESS_BENCH_CONFIGS=pi__llamacpp__qwen3.8-flash-next-125b-a6b-udiq4xs \
#   HARNESS_CONFIRM=1 make coding-bench
#
# The same against the managed gufo (item 12 after item 60's cut-over). Pi's
# bundle defaults to gufo's; the run refuses when gufo is not the one serving:
#   HARNESS_BENCH_ENGINE_MODE=managed HARNESS_BENCH_EXPECT_ENGINE=gufo \
#   HARNESS_BENCH_SUITES=swe-bench-lite \
#   HARNESS_BENCH_CONFIGS=pi__gufo__qwen3.8-flash-next-125b-a6b-udq4kxl \
#   HARNESS_CONFIRM=1 make coding-bench
#
# --- SWE-bench Pro (item 37.1, ADR 0023) ------------------------------------
# All optional; the defaults are the pins. Graded by Harbor's patch_replay on a
# pristine image (scripts/harness/bench/swe-bench-pro.sh). The grader proof is
#   HARNESS_CONFIRM=1 make coding-bench-pro-proof
# BENCH_HARBOR_BIN=        # default ~/.local/share/ai-sdlc-tools/harbor-0.22.0/bin/harbor
# BENCH_HARBOR_VERSION=    # default 0.22.0 (the upstream release gate's Harbor)
# BENCH_HARBOR_TOOL_DIR=   # where `make coding-bench-pro-proof` installs it
# BENCH_SWEBENCHPRO_ROOT=  # default ~/.cache/ai-sdlc-bench/swe-bench-pro (task tree + jobs)
# BENCH_SWEBENCHPRO_UPSTREAM=   # the SWE-bench_Pro-os checkout; default <ROOT>/SWE-bench_Pro-os
# BENCH_SWEBENCHPRO_PREDICTIONS=  # unset = the agent; gold | empty prove the grader alone
# BENCH_SWEBENCHPRO_DISK_GB=      # default 200, provisional until item 37.1 T2 measures

# --- Dev-time prerequisites for a LIVE run ----------------------------------
# Installed by the OPERATOR at the pinned versions, never vendored and never a
# repo dependency (the `lizard` precedent). `make check` needs NONE of them.
#   swebench==5.0.2         SWE-bench Lite      (+ Docker, ~120 GB of images)
#   harbor==0.22.0          SWE-bench Pro       (+ Docker + compose; no [modal])
#   evalplus==0.3.1         HumanEval+ / MBPP+  (+ Docker for sandboxed grading)
#   bigcodebench==0.2.5     BigCodeBench-Hard   (+ Docker)
#   LiveCodeBench @ 28fef95ea8c9   set BENCH_LIVECODEBENCH_HOME to the checkout
#   opencode 1.17.12 · mini-swe-agent 2.4.6     the two agent harnesses
#   ollama 0.31.2 · llama-server                the two engines

# --- Langfuse observability (item 15.1; scripts/langfuse/) -------------------
# Self-hosted Langfuse for harness traces. `make langfuse-up` starts it,
# generates any missing secret below into YOUR .env (chmod 600, never
# overwriting), and waits for health. `make check` needs none of this: the
# selftest mocks docker and curl.
#
# Where the stack runs. Empty, or a host that is THIS machine (loopback, one of
# its interface addresses such as the EVO-X3's own 192.168.1.57, or a name
# resolving to one) = this machine is the SERVER (item 28). Point it at another
# host (e.g. http://192.168.1.50:3000) to act as a CLIENT: up/down/destroy
# then refuse and say why, and status/env/compact/watermark work over the API
# against that server (item 15.2 deploys the EVO-X3 server).
LANGFUSE_HOST=""
# Address the web port is published on. 127.0.0.1 keeps it off the network.
# ON THE EVO-X3 SERVER this is its LAN IP (192.168.1.57) — a specific address,
# never 0.0.0.0, so the port is on the LAN and nowhere else. Data services are
# NEVER published, on any address. Port numbers live in scripts/langfuse/ports.env.
LANGFUSE_BIND="127.0.0.1"
LANGFUSE_PORT="3000"
# What `make langfuse-client-env` hands to other machines. Only needed when it
# differs from LANGFUSE_BIND (e.g. bound to 0.0.0.0 behind a reverse proxy);
# otherwise the bind address is used, or the primary route's IP is guessed.
LANGFUSE_LAN_IP=""
# LAN subnet the DOCKER-USER firewall rule allows through to the published
# port. Docker bypasses ufw, so this rule is the only thing bounding it:
#   make langfuse-firewall      # prints the rules; an operator applies them
LANGFUSE_LAN_SUBNET="192.168.1.0/24"
# How often the systemd user timer runs compaction (make langfuse-timer-install).
LANGFUSE_TIMER_MINUTES="15"
# How often the ensure timer checks the stack and restarts it if a service is
# down (make langfuse-ensure-timer-install). The first check runs 2 min after
# boot. A stack taken down with `make langfuse-down` is left down.
LANGFUSE_ENSURE_MINUTES="5"
# Where `up` stages ClickHouse's config (compose binds it from here) and where
# `down` leaves the intended-down marker. It is outside every checkout on
# purpose: a bind into a removed git worktree left ClickHouse unstartable after
# a reboot (item 27). Must be absolute. Empty = ${XDG_STATE_HOME:-~/.local/state}/ai-sdlc-langfuse.
LANGFUSE_STATE_DIR=""
#
# Memory budget in MiB for the whole stack. `status` warns above it, and the
# selftest asserts that compose.yml's mem_limit sum equals it.
# MEASURED on the EVO-X3 (item 15.2 T7/T8, 2026-09-20): a 2.01 h agent run with
# the 94 GB incumbent served alongside peaked at 1958 MiB with no OOM kill and
# no swap growth. Each limit is its measured peak plus a stated headroom, or
# the service's own heap/server ceiling where that is higher — see compose.yml.
# The planned 2048 remains infeasible: web alone OOM-kills at 832 MiB.
# 2816 -> 3392 (item 61): ClickHouse's server cap 450 -> 1024 MiB, the user's
# 1,024 MiB ceiling (formula gave 1,088), and its container 576 -> 1152 MiB (cap + 128).
LANGFUSE_BUDGET_MIB="3392"
#
# Preflight thresholds. `up` refuses below these; set LANGFUSE_IGNORE_PREFLIGHT=1
# to override (recorded in the log, never silent).
LANGFUSE_MIN_FREE_RAM_GB="3"
LANGFUSE_MIN_FREE_DISK_GB="10"
LANGFUSE_IGNORE_PREFLIGHT=""
#
# Compaction. Old traces are archived to gzipped JSONL and only then deleted.
# Presets live in scripts/langfuse/compaction.presets: dev | bench | archive-all.
LANGFUSE_COMPACT_PRESET="dev"
LANGFUSE_ARCHIVE_DIR="results/langfuse-archive"
LANGFUSE_COMPACT_DRY_RUN=""
# Archive and verify, delete nothing: how an archive is reviewed before the
# first deleting run (item 35). The dry run wins over it.
LANGFUSE_COMPACT_NO_DELETE=""
# The compaction timer installs with deletion OFF (a dry-run drop-in). Set to 1
# AT INSTALL, once the archive has been reviewed, to let it delete:
#   LANGFUSE_COMPACT_TIMER_DELETE=1 make langfuse-timer-install
LANGFUSE_COMPACT_TIMER_DELETE=""
#
# Write mode for the v4 images: legacy | dual | events_only. `legacy` keeps the
# v3-shaped API that compaction reads through; the cutover is 15.3's decision
# (scripts/langfuse/PINS.md). CHANGE THESE TWO TOGETHER: v4 refuses to start on
# legacy + direct ("Invalid V4 config"), because direct writes to a table that
# legacy does not read.
LANGFUSE_WRITE_MODE="legacy"
LANGFUSE_OTEL_BEHAVIOUR="dual_write"
#
# Ingest health (`make langfuse-check-ingest`, also reported by langfuse-status).
# GRACE_S: how far the newest trace may trail the newest observation before the
# watermark is called stale — one long interactive session legitimately trails
# its own observations by hours, so this is not a tight bound. TRACES: how many
# of the newest traces are checked for a second root observation, which is the
# direct signature of a collided trace id. MAX_PAGES caps the observation pages
# read per trace (100 rows each).
LANGFUSE_INGEST_GRACE_S="3600"
LANGFUSE_INGEST_TRACES="5"
LANGFUSE_INGEST_MAX_PAGES="10"
#
# How long compaction waits for asynchronous deletes to land before stopping.
# Stopping early is normal — the next run continues where this one left off.
LANGFUSE_DELETE_LAG_S="300"
#
# Generated on first `up` if empty — do not hand-edit, and never commit them.
LANGFUSE_PUBLIC_KEY=""
LANGFUSE_SECRET_KEY=""
LANGFUSE_SALT=""
LANGFUSE_ENCRYPTION_KEY=""
LANGFUSE_NEXTAUTH_SECRET=""
LANGFUSE_POSTGRES_PASSWORD=""
LANGFUSE_CLICKHOUSE_PASSWORD=""
LANGFUSE_REDIS_PASSWORD=""
LANGFUSE_MINIO_PASSWORD=""
# The web UI login for the headlessly created user (local@ai-sdlc.invalid).
LANGFUSE_INIT_USER_PASSWORD=""
# Destroy is irreversible (it deletes the volumes), so it is gated:
#   LANGFUSE_CONFIRM_DESTROY=1 make langfuse-destroy
LANGFUSE_CONFIRM_DESTROY=""

# --- Pi remote control, the push target (item 98.1, ADR 0040) ----------------
# Where scripts/langfuse/pi-gufo-local/push.js sends its notification when Pi
# settles or blocks on a human. BOTH are read from THIS FILE ONLY, never from the
# environment: Pi's bash tool hands its own environment to every command, so a
# token exported into the shell is one `env` call away from the model, the
# transcript and the trace. The installer bakes the MAIN checkout's absolute path
# into the installed copy, so this file is the one that is read even from a
# worktree (ADR 0014) — a worktree's own .env is never used for this.
#
# THE TOPIC IS A CREDENTIAL. Anyone who knows an ntfy topic can read every message
# on it, and the messages carry the command Pi is asking about. Pick a long random
# topic, keep it in this file, and never commit it or print it.
#
# The push is OFF unless AISDLC_PI_PUSH=on is set in the shell that starts
# pi-gufo; with both keys unset it registers no handler at all. The service that
# answers this URL is item 98.2's, and nothing here starts or assumes one.
# AISDLC_PI_PUSH_URL="http://127.0.0.1:8099"
# AISDLC_PI_PUSH_TOPIC="pick-a-long-random-string"

# --- Held-out benchmark (item 38.1, ADR 0016) --------------------------------
# Where the held-out benchmark's instances live: an ABSOLUTE path to a
# directory OUTSIDE every git checkout, e.g. ~/.local/share/ai-sdlc-heldout.
# An instance (task statement, gold and test patches, FAIL_TO_PASS list) must
# never be tracked in this public repository, so scripts/heldout/ refuses
# (exit 3) when this is unset, relative, missing, or inside a checkout. Only
# ids, hashes, counts and aggregate scores ever cross into the repository.
# Create the directory yourself; nothing creates it for you.
# HELDOUT_STORE="/home/you/.local/share/ai-sdlc-heldout"
#
# Item 38.2: the harvester and the runner (scripts/heldout/harvest.sh, run.sh).
# The seconds one selftest may run inside the oracle before it is cut off, in
# discovery and in grading:
# HELDOUT_RUNNER_TIMEOUT_S="1200"
# What `make heldout-run` drives: the harness adapter under
# scripts/harness/bench/agents/, the MANAGED pinned server's base URL (never
# started or stopped by the run), and the model id it answers to:
# HELDOUT_RUN_HARNESS="pi"
# HELDOUT_RUN_ENDPOINT="http://127.0.0.1:18080"
# HELDOUT_RUN_MODEL="qwen3.8-flash-next"
